                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the portfolio: demand, capacity and margin`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Three proposals, three different shapes. <strong>Program A (Business Analytics)</strong> is high-price ($52k) but capacity-hungry — 22 senior-faculty sections for 60 seats. <strong>Program B (Health Admin)</strong> is high-volume (90 seats, $38k) with many sections adjunct-eligible at $32k. <strong>Program C (Sustainable Finance)</strong> is prestigious but capacity-inefficient — 26 senior sections for just 30 seats. They are <em>not comparable on a single axis</em>. With faculty sections as the binding constraint, the sharpest prioritization metric is contribution-margin <strong>per scarce section</strong> — not sticker price, not headline demand. Run the funnel (≈55% admit × ≈38% enroll) and the section-cost math before you rank.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Prioritize under the capacity ceiling`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `You have <strong>45 free sections</strong>; the three programs demand <strong>78</strong>. Something must be cut, phased or shrunk. Launch two at full scale and phase one? Launch all three sub-scale (and risk none reaching a viable cohort)? Build the capacity ledger — sections by program and faculty type — and decide your <strong>adjunct vs senior-faculty mix</strong>. The trap: quietly assuming you can <em>find</em> the missing sections. Overflow comes from somewhere — disclosed adjunct overload at a quality cost, or sections pulled from the flagship MBA, whose NPS and rankings underwrite the whole school.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate the budget and set tuition`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Route the fixed <strong>$1,200,000</strong> to where each dollar buys the most enrolled students. Program B must fill 90 seats (≈430 applications); Program C needs only ≈140. <em>Equal thirds over-funds the small cohort and starves the large one.</em> Then set tuition under <strong>price–yield elasticity</strong>: raising price lifts per-student contribution but depresses yield — push price +15% while holding the 38% yield assumption constant and your cohorts shrink and classrooms sit half-empty at full faculty cost. Spend where it converts; price where the yield holds.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the portfolio to the Board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present the approved portfolio to the Dean and a skeptical Trustee as <strong>one integrated decision</strong> — which programs, at what scale and price, with what marketing, filling what seats at what contribution margin per section — and what you deliberately chose <em>not</em> to do, and why. Address capacity fill, blended portfolio margin and the projected student-satisfaction impact. The trap: presenting it as upside only. The Trustee will raise the downside — a July <em>visa freeze</em> halving Program A's international cohort. Bring a named contingency, or the Board hears a sure thing that isn't.`,

