                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_persection:    `<strong>Right metric.</strong> When faculty sections are the binding constraint, the question is value extracted per scarce section — contribution-margin <strong>per section</strong>, not per student. That is what lets you compare a 60-seat premium program against a 90-seat volume one on the same axis.`,
                    coach_r1_totalmargin:   `<strong>Total margin is closer, but it hides the constraint.</strong> A program with big absolute dollars can still be a poor use of scarce sections. Divide by sections consumed — the denominator that matters here is faculty time, not headcount.`,
                    coach_r1_sticker:       `<strong>Program A bills $52,000 a head, so you launched it first?</strong> But it eats 22 of your 45 sections, mostly at $95k senior-faculty cost. Show me its contribution margin per section versus Program B's adjunct-taught sections. Price per student is not value per constraint.`,
                    coach_r1_demand:        `<strong>Headline demand is a trap here.</strong> Health Admin has the biggest market, but throughput is limited by faculty sections, not applicants. A program can be in huge demand and still be a poor use of the constraint. Rank by margin per scarce section.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You chose contribution-margin-per-section AND ranked Program B first — its adjunct-eligible sections return the most value per unit of the binding constraint. That is exactly the lens that survives the Trustee's challenge in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r1_ranka:         `<strong>Program A is your highest sticker price, but not your best margin per section.</strong> Twenty-two sections at $95k senior-faculty cost for 60 seats is capacity-expensive. Program B's adjunct-eligible sections return more value per scarce section. Re-rank on the constraint, not the price tag.`,
                    coach_r1_rankc:         `<strong>Program C is the most capacity-inefficient of the three.</strong> Twenty-six senior-faculty sections for only 30 seats is a low margin per scarce section, however prestigious. It can be a strategic add — but it is not your #1 on the constraint.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>You ranked without running the math.</strong> Without the funnel (≈55% admit × ≈38% enroll) and the cost-per-section table, your ranking is intuition. The whole portfolio decision rests on numbers you have not yet computed.`,
                    coach_r2_twostrong:     `<strong>That is a real prioritization decision.</strong> Two programs at full scale within the ceiling, one cleanly phased to 2027 — each launch reaches a viable cohort and you have not raided the flagship. This is what 'deciding' looks like, versus spreading yourself thin.`,
                    coach_r2_premiumpair:   `<strong>You launched the two premium programs and phased B — but B was your best margin per scarce section.</strong> A and C are the two most capacity-expensive proposals; together they push you toward 48 sections. You phased the program that returned the most value per unit of the constraint.`,
                    coach_r2_allthree:      `<strong>You shrank all three to fit.</strong> Now none reaches the cohort size where it covers its fixed program costs, and your marketing and faculty are spread thin across three sub-scale launches. Is three weak programs better than two strong ones? Avoiding the choice has a cost.`,
                    coach_r2_overcommit:    `<strong>Your plan needs 78 sections. You have 45.</strong> The other 33 come from somewhere — adjunct overload at a quality cost, or sections pulled from your flagship MBA. When the MBA's NPS drops because its best faculty are teaching your new launch, who explains that to the Trustees?`,
                    coach_r2_phantom:       `<strong>Phantom capacity.</strong> Your ledger exceeds 45 sections but you claimed it fits. The constraint is real — overflow has a hidden bill. Disclose where the extra sections come from, or re-scope until you are honestly inside the ceiling.`,
                    coach_r2_raid:          `<strong>Raiding the flagship MBA is the hidden bill.</strong> Its best faculty now teach your new launch; next spring its NPS and rankings inputs drop, degrading students already enrolled. That is the most expensive way to 'find' capacity — and the Board will see it.`,
                    coach_r2_adjunctbuy:    `<strong>Disclosed adjunct overload is at least honest</strong> — you named the overflow and its quality cost rather than hiding it. It is still capacity you did not have; a cleaner cut would have avoided buying overflow at all.`,
                    coach_r2_adjunctheavy:  `<strong>Careful — adjunct-heavy everywhere erodes the premium programs.</strong> Program A and C need senior-faculty depth; staffing nearly everything with adjuncts saves money but degrades the very programs whose price and prestige justify them.`,
                    coach_r2_allsenior:     `<strong>All-senior-faculty staffing burns margin and capacity.</strong> Where the curriculum allows adjuncts at $32k versus $95k, using them lifts contribution margin per section and stretches your 45 sections further. You left cheaper, viable capacity on the table.`,
                    coach_r3_yieldweighted: `<strong>Yield-driven allocation.</strong> You routed the most marketing to Program B, which must fill 90 seats on ≈430 applications, and the least to the small boutique C. Each dollar went where it buys the most enrolled students — not where it felt fair.`,
                    coach_r3_overfundsmall: `<strong>You over-funded the small cohort.</strong> Program C needs only ≈140 applications for 30 seats; pouring budget there buys few extra students while the 90-seat Program B sits under-filled. Move dollars to where the seats — and the applications needed — are largest.`,
                    coach_r3_equalsplit:    `<strong>You split the budget into equal thirds.</strong> But B needs ≈430 applications and C needs ≈140 — equal dollars over-fund the small cohort and under-fund the large one, so B under-enrolls and you leave margin on the table. Allocate by yield, not by fairness.`,
                    coach_r3_holdsharp:     `<strong>Disciplined.</strong> Yield-weighted spend AND an honest price — holding modeled tuition keeps your 38% yield assumption real, so your enrollment projection is defensible to the Dean rather than a number you wished into existence.`,
                    coach_r3_premium:       `<strong>You pushed price up 15% across the board.</strong> But your model assumes 38% yield at the old price. Move price and that assumption is fiction — fewer admits enroll, cohorts shrink, and sections sit half-empty at full faculty cost. What is your evidence that demand holds at this price?`,
                    coach_r3_discount:      `<strong>Deep discounting fills seats but erodes margin.</strong> A 20% cut guarantees full classrooms while gutting contribution margin per section — and in a capacity-constrained school, a full but unprofitable program is a poor use of scarce faculty time.`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>That is a Board-ready frame.</strong> Programs, scale, price, margin per section, and an explicit statement of what you chose not to do — that is a portfolio decision a Trustee can interrogate and trust, not a top-line headline.`,
                    coach_r4_revenue:       `<strong>A top-line revenue story invites the obvious question.</strong> Total tuition and headcount say nothing about value per scarce section or whether the cohorts actually fill. The Trustee will ask for the margin math you are not showing.`,
                    coach_r4_prestige:      `<strong>Prestige alone will not survive the Trustee.</strong> Rankings and donor appeal are real, but a portfolio defended on marquee value with no margin-per-section discipline reads as the sticker-price error dressed up. Lead with the economics.`,
                    coach_r4_vaguecut:      `<strong>Strong frame, but 'revisit later' is not a defended cut.</strong> Name the program you phased, quantify the margin you forwent this cycle, and make the case to revisit it in 2027 — that is what makes the cut credible rather than evasive.`,
                    coach_r4_nocut:         `<strong>You presented the launches as upside only.</strong> Prioritization means deciding — and a portfolio with no acknowledged cut looks like you never confronted the 45-section ceiling. What did the program you forwent actually cost the school, and why was that the right call?`,
                    coach_r4_monitor:       `<strong>'We'll monitor and react' is not a contingency.</strong> If the July visa freeze lands, half of Program A's international admits cannot enroll and your STEM flagship opens at 40% capacity with full faculty committed. Name the backstop before the Board, not after.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>You presented Program A's international fill as a sure thing.</strong> A visa freeze lands in July; half your international admits cannot enroll, and your premium launch opens half-empty with faculty committed for two years. You owed the Board the downside — and a plan for it.`,
                    coach_r4_boardready:    `<strong>Board-ready.</strong> An integrated decision, a defended cut, and a named downside contingency — domestic backstop, deferred intake, capacity reflow to B. That is a portfolio the Trustees can approve with their eyes open.`,

